<h2>How the question reached the Court</h2>
<p>In May 2026, the Supreme Court decided <em>M/s Mansi Finance (Chennai) Ltd. v. M. Lalitha</em>, a quashing matter under Sections 138 and 141 of the Negotiable Instruments Act concerning vicarious liability of persons in charge of a company. The judgment partly allowed the appeal and restored proceedings against three of the four accused. It said nothing, in terms, about State money-lending statutes.</p>
<p>That silence did not prevent the judgment from acquiring a reputation it never claimed. An applicant — a third party facing proceedings of his own — moved a miscellaneous application asking the Court to "clarify" that the judgment permits unlicensed moneylenders across India to maintain Section 138 complaints over loans advanced against negotiable instruments. On 29 October 2026, the Bench of Justices Prashant Kumar Mishra and N.V. Anjaria declined.</p>

<h2>What the Court actually held</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>No pan-India reading.</strong> The May 2026 judgment dealt with quashing and vicarious liability; it did not examine either the Tamil Nadu Money-Lenders Act, 1957 or the Maharashtra Money-Lending (Regulation) Act, 2014. There was therefore nothing to clarify, and no countrywide licence-free charter to be extracted from it.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Maharashtra is differently worded.</strong> Unlike the Tamil Nadu Act, the Maharashtra Act excludes from the definition of "loan" only advances against negotiable instruments <em>other than a promissory note</em>. An interest-bearing advance against a promissory note is therefore squarely a regulated loan in Maharashtra.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Sections 4 and 13 bite.</strong> Section 4 of the Maharashtra Act prohibits unlicensed money-lending, and Section 13 renders such loans unenforceable. A debt the statute refuses to enforce may not be a "legally enforceable debt" within the Explanation to Section 138 NI Act.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Licence at the threshold.</strong> The Court recalled its earlier order of 6 April 2026 (M.A. 1176/2026 in SLP (Crl.) 5485/2024), under which proceedings by private moneylenders should be halted at the outset unless the lender produces a licence or shows the money was not advanced at interest in the course of money-lending business.</div>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>The operative outcome is deliberately modest: the application was disposed of with observations, and any plea that Section 13 of the Maharashtra Act bars a particular proceeding "must be examined by the concerned court on its own merits". The Court clarified the limits of its precedent without deciding individual prosecutions.</p>
</div>

<h2>Why the Explanation to Section 138 matters</h2>
<p>Section 138 punishes dishonour of a cheque drawn "for the discharge, in whole or in part, of any debt or other liability", and the Explanation confines that phrase to a <em>legally enforceable</em> debt or liability. The presumption under Section 139 helps the complainant prove the existence of the debt; it does not transform an unenforceable debt into an enforceable one. Where a State legislature has said that an unlicensed lender's loan cannot be enforced, the criminal consequence under Section 138 can fall with the civil enforceability — which is precisely why the threshold production of a licence becomes decisive in States with prohibitory statutes.</p>

<h2>The practical map for litigants</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Identify the State statute.</strong> Money-lending regulation is State-specific. The Maharashtra Act of 2014 and the Tamil Nadu Act of 1957 are differently drafted, and other States differ again. The first question in any private-financier cheque case is which Act, if any, governs the advance.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Characterise the advance.</strong> The statutes regulate loans advanced at interest in the course of money-lending business. A one-off friendly advance, trade credit, or a transaction outside the statutory definition of "loan" stands on a different footing from systematic financing.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Raise or meet the licence point early.</strong> For the accused, the plea belongs at the threshold — in reply to summoning, in an application before the Magistrate, or in a quashing petition where the defect is apparent. For the complainant, the licence, or material showing the statute does not apply, should accompany the complaint.</div>
</div>

<h2>What the order does not decide</h2>
<p>Three limits deserve emphasis. First, the Court did not quash any prosecution; it left enforceability to be tested case by case. Second, the observations concern regulated moneylenders, not banks or non-banking financial companies operating under their own regulatory regimes. Third, nothing in the order dilutes the borrower's burden to displace the statutory presumptions under Sections 118 and 139 NI Act on the facts — the licence question is an additional gateway, not a substitute for the ordinary defences.</p>
<p>For Delhi practitioners, the order is a reminder that cheque bounce litigation is not a self-contained code: the enforceability of the underlying debt, tested against State regulatory statutes, can decide the case before the presumptions are ever reached. Advice on either side of such a complaint should begin with the lender's regulatory position, not end with it.</p>
